[yossi@Neon.Stanford.EDU (Joseph Friedman) writes:]
>[kevin@cfctech.UUCP (Kevin Darcy) writes:]
>>Please speak up if you have an opinion on this whole poly-pile issue...
>Is the game really that easy with a wand of polymorph?  I figure ...
>My vote is *NOT* to restrict the polymorphing trick.  Those who feel
>the game is too easy should play as healers, or tourists, for challenge
>(maybe start over a few times until you get a weak enough character,
>with initial strength 7 :-)  Try to get through the first few levels!
.
hmmm... consider this: with a poly-wand you can easily obtain AC -40
that in addition provides 2x speed, non-blind telepathy, extra strength
or dexterity, reflection, magic resistance, protection and so on. also,
you can have stats of 18 (or better) across the board. named weapons,
a couple of life saving amulets, no problem. in case you forgot
something or want something in a pinch you'll need a couple of w.o.w.'s.
all that and more (practically unlimited) from a poly-wand (upto pl6)...

i'm tempted to post my discovery list which resulted from poly-piling
during a 10M game, maybe sometime later ...

suggesting that "poly-experts" play weak characters to get a challenging
game doesn't really solve the problem. the problem isn't the starting
game challenge, rather it's the loophole created by the fact that
polymorph is not problematic. simply put, you can turn garbage to your
advantage and create increasingly more magic in the process! i think
no one will argue against that feature being amended. imagine the
reluctance to use the "polymorph pile" stunt if on occasion a polymorph
demon would emerge and randomly scramble you and your inventory...

let polymorph be polymorph, which includes the risk that if a thing
undergoes a shape-change it faces the possibility of disintegrating.
this approach doesn't handcuff the "poly-novice" and makes it
"statistically" unlikely that a "poly-expert" can abuse the privilege.
that seems fair ...

